The result is correct, I don't see any bug there. Seems like you're confusing the values of Vector2 constants (`Vector2.Up == Vector2(0, -1)`.

For:
```csharp
var mesh = new ImmediateMesh();
mesh.SurfaceBegin(Mesh.PrimitiveType.TriangleStrip);

mesh.SurfaceSetUV(Vector2.Zero);
mesh.SurfaceAddVertex2D(Vector2.Zero);

mesh.SurfaceSetUV(Vector2.Down);
mesh.SurfaceAddVertex2D(Vector2.Down);

mesh.SurfaceSetUV(Vector2.Right);
mesh.SurfaceAddVertex2D(Vector2.Right);

mesh.SurfaceSetUV(Vector2.One);
mesh.SurfaceAddVertex2D(Vector2.One);

mesh.SurfaceEnd();
```

Result:
- with your custom material (hence inverted UVs):
<img width="83" height="97" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/38d1952e-db97-43a4-a537-878a65ecf134" />

- without custom material:
<img width="83" height="97" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/53a24e23-c12d-4e1e-928a-1d540299ca37" />


Regarding the interpolation, the MRP above has physics interpolation turned off, hence it's irrelevant for the presented test case.
It might be the case that you're missing `Node.reset_physics_interpolation()` call in your project, or something alike (be sure to check [relevant docs](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/4.6/tutorials/physics/interpolation/index.html)).
